# GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964 (msgpack Unpacker out-of-bounds read/crash)
# msgpack here is vendored inside pip itself (pip/_vendor/msgpack), pulled in
# by `pip install --upgrade pip` in the Dockerfile. It is not a project
# dependency - app/ never imports msgpack, and pip's vendored copy is only
# used internally by pip's own resolver/cache, not reachable at runtime.
# No requirements.txt/Dockerfile change can patch a version vendored inside
# pip's own release; revisit if a future pip release bundles a fixed copy.
GHSA-6v7p-g79w-8964

# CVE-2025-47273 (setuptools Path Traversal in PackageIndex), reported as
# setuptools 70.3.0.
# Verified false positive: the running image's actual installed setuptools
# is 84.0.0 (`pip show setuptools` and
# `python3 -c "import setuptools; print(setuptools.__version__)"` both
# confirm this), and `find / -iname '*setuptools*70.3*'` across the full
# built container filesystem returns nothing - 70.3.0 does not exist
# anywhere in the image. Reproduced across multiple base image patch tags
# (python:3.10-slim, 3.10.16/17/18/19-slim) with the same result each time:
# a different real installed setuptools version in every case, never
# 70.3.0. Our Dockerfile always upgrades setuptools to >=78.1.1 regardless
# of the base image's starting version. Trivy appears to misattribute this
# finding, likely by parsing a `setuptools>=70.3.0`-style minimum-version
# build constraint from some other package's metadata as if it were an
# installed version. Re-verify if the Trivy version changes.
CVE-2025-47273
